From a6a9b8b927d992bf55c47426143820075630ca3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Feder <noreply@splitmind.invalid>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:51:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat(mcp-oauth): budget client registration, owner-only
 sign-in

Workers Free allows 1,000 KV writes a day, and unauthenticated requests
could use them up and stop token refresh for connected clients. Three
requests wrote to OAUTH_KV: POST /oauth/mcp/register, GET
/oauth/mcp/authorize (a consent transaction, reachable with an
attacker-hosted CIMD client that needs no registration), and POST
/oauth/mcp/token with a client_assertion (a replay marker).

Registration: when a Durable Object namespace is bound as
MCP_OAUTH_ANONYMOUS_WRITES (class McpOAuthAnonymousWriteBudget,
SQLite-backed), a registration takes a slot after the redirect allowlist
passes: at most OOMOL_CONNECT_MCP_OAUTH_MAX_ANONYMOUS_WRITES_PER_DAY
(default 100) in total and ..._PER_IP_PER_DAY (default 20) per
CF-Connecting-IP (IPv6 per /64), per UTC day, both checked and taken in
one call under blockConcurrencyWhile. Over the budget, registration
answers invalid_client_metadata with Retry-After set to the seconds until
00:00 UTC, with no KV write, and logs one console.warn that names neither
the address nor the client. If the budget errors, or the per-request
scope is missing, the registration is refused. Without the binding there
is no registration cap.

Sign-in: GET /oauth/mcp/authorize validates the request and renders the
consent form without beginConsent, so it writes nothing, sets no cookie
and has no handle. The form posts back to the same URL. The POST
re-validates the request from its own URL, checks the administrator token
for Allow and for Deny, then calls completeAuthorization (Allow) or
redirects with authorizationErrorRedirect (Deny). The authorize path
never touches the budget.

Token endpoint: a request with client_assertion or client_assertion_type
gets 401 invalid_client (read from a clone, one console.warn with no
client_id), and a urlencoded body over 64 KiB gets 400. Both run with or
without the binding. The wrapper's own responses carry the library's CORS
headers.
